From 2ed3401cc4c2f25ed29021056bb8f1093994709b Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Hades <15710513+hadesdevls@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Wed, 28 Oct 2026 20:49:33 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] windowing/gbm: Dolby Vision for the menus
 (videoscreen.hadesdvmenus, off by default)

With a display that takes Dolby Vision Standard and the menus at 3840x2160, every change between the
menus and a Dolby Vision video makes the TV switch its picture mode into Dolby Vision and back, a
blackout of a second or more each time. With this setting on, the menus go out in the tunnel of
native output while no video plays, so that a native Dolby Vision video takes the output over
without the TV changing its picture mode, and gives it back at its end the same way. The idea is
YBLOD's (0.3.0); this is written again for Hades' window system:

- A frame of the menus is drawn only when Kodi draws its GUI: CompositeGui() composites the GUI into
  the black picture of a frame of graphics (CDVBridgeGLES::PrepareGraphics()) as it composites it
  over a native video, at the GUI white of videoscreen.hadesguiwhite, and PresentDVMenus() packs it
  and commits it the way that waits. Otherwise the display keeps the last one.
- The moves between them use what native output has for two videos in a row: a native video that
  opens at the menus' rate takes the output over (SetVideoOutput(), CDVModeMove::HandsOver()); one at
  another rate moves the tunnel into its mode, which the next frame carries (CDVModeMove::Moves(),
  which also lets the menus go on into a mode of the menus, never into that of a video that does not
  go out natively); at its end the output goes back to the menus (EndDVBridgeAfterVideo(),
  DVMenusAfterVideo()) and moves into the menus' mode. Any other video ends the menus' tunnel with its
  change of mode in one commit, or before its first picture; the menus go out in Dolby Vision again
  once nothing of it is left on the output. That is two more changes of the TV's picture mode for
  each such video, which is why it is a setting, off by default.
- Fail closed (CDVMenus): they do not start with Low-Latency or the HDMI 1.4b signalling, partial
  redraws, a stereoscopic GUI, or while anything of a video is left; they end when the setting or
  native output is turned off, the display is put to sleep or the system wakes, a game plays, or the
  display sends a hot-plug event, after which it is looked at again (CheckDisplayChange(), now under
  a lock shared with DVNewVideo()) before they start again. A frame that cannot be prepared, packed or
  committed, or whose flips do not come, ends them, and they are tried again after 30 s, then 5
  minutes, then only after a change of the display or of the setting. Nothing of theirs disables
  native output for videos. They never choose a mode: a video keeps the cadence it would have.
- What native output sets up when it starts and leaves when it ends is now in functions of their own
  (DVNativeOutputBegins(), DVNativeOutputEnds()), the same code where it was, so that a video that
  takes the output from the menus, or gives it back, starts and ends as a native output does.
- kodi.log says when they go out, with their white and metadata, why they do not, why they end, and
  each hand-over; the Dolby Vision page of the player information has a row "Menus in Dolby Vision"
  (Estuary and Hades' own dialog). The setting's label and help are in English, translated in
  Estuary for es_es, es_mx and es_ar.

Checked on the host: the decisions of CDVMenus, CDVModeMove::Moves() unchanged in all 1024
combinations of its fields without the menus, and a model of the window system that counts, for one
video played from menus at 2160p60, two changes of the link and none of the picture mode for Dolby
Vision at 23.976 Hz (two and two without the setting), none for Dolby Vision at 60 Hz, four and four
for HDR10 at 23.976 Hz (two and two without). The sources compile with the commands of the 1.0 RC4
build. Nothing of this has run on a GPU or been seen on a TV.
