diff --git a/addons/resource.language.en_gb/resources/strings.po b/addons/resource.language.en_gb/resources/strings.po
index 3ae8050..a7fa9bd 100644
--- a/addons/resource.language.en_gb/resources/strings.po
+++ b/addons/resource.language.en_gb/resources/strings.po
@@ -26703,3 +26703,15 @@ msgstr ""
 msgctxt "#40927"
 msgid "For a display that takes HDR10+ and not Dolby Vision, as many Samsung TVs. A Dolby Vision video rebuilt as HDR10 is sent as HDR10+: with each scene, dynamic metadata that Hades measures from the rebuilt picture (the average and the distribution of its brightness), so that the display adapts each scene as it adapts an HDR10+ film. The scenes are those of the Dolby Vision metadata. These metadata are not the studio's, and no tone curve is sent: the display makes its own. Profile 8.1 is then rebuilt too; profile 7 as the setting above says. The first picture of a scene waits for its own measurement where it comes before the next refresh, else it goes out with the metadata of the scene before; the display may show a scene otherwise than as plain HDR10. Not while the picture is mapped to the display. Applies from the next video."
 msgstr ""
+
+#. Hades: label of setting "videoscreen.hadesdvmenus"
+#: system/settings/settings.xml
+msgctxt "#40990"
+msgid "Dolby Vision for the menus"
+msgstr ""
+
+#. Hades: help of setting "videoscreen.hadesdvmenus"
+#: system/settings/settings.xml
+msgctxt "#40991"
+msgid "Keeps the TV in Dolby Vision while the menus are shown: a Dolby Vision video then starts without the TV changing its picture mode, and goes back to the menus the same way. Any other video (SDR, HDR10, HDR10+, HLG) makes the TV change its mode once more at its start and once more at its end. The menus are sent with fixed metadata, at the white of \"GUI and subtitle brightness in HDR\" (203 cd/m² at 100 %), and the TV shows them in its Dolby Vision picture mode, which may look different from its SDR one. While the menus change, the graphics chip converts each of their frames. Shown only for a display that takes Dolby Vision, with the menus at 3840x2160. If the TV shows a black or broken picture, turn this off."
+msgstr ""
